Who Controls the Nile? — the Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam

Style: Satellite Briefing, from Lemo-Opuscar by Lemomo (github.com/lemomo-ai/lemo-opuscar). Camera maths (top of engine.js) from the
  style's demo; the imagery shader, film, measurements and mix are this film's own. Code: MIT.
  If published, the description should credit: "Style: Lemo-Opuscar by Lemomo (CC BY 4.0)".
Imagery (public domain): NASA Blue Marble: Next Generation and VIIRS Black Marble 2016 via NASA GIBS; USGS Landsat Collection 2
  (Landsat 8 and 9 OLI), courtesy of the U.S. Geological Survey, via the Microsoft Planetary Computer. Scene IDs in
  assets/plates/plates.json and assets/measure.json; every request in assets/raw/manifest.jsonl. No borders are drawn.
Elevation: NASADEM (NASA/JPL, public domain) via the Microsoft Planetary Computer. The 3D canyon is the 2000 terrain; the water
  is a flat plane at the published level of each filling, shown only inside the shoreline measured on Landsat in January 2026.
Rivers: Natural Earth 1:10m river centrelines (public domain, naturalearthdata.com).
Voice: Kokoro-82M (Apache-2.0), voice bm_george.
Music samples (CC0): VS Chamber Orchestra Community Edition and Versilian Community Sample Library (vibraphone, timpani) via the
  library's sampler.py. Pad and pulse: in code. Ambience: Mixkit "river water flowing" (free licence for videos).
Fonts (SIL OFL 1.1): Barlow, Barlow Condensed, IBM Plex Mono, IBM Plex Serif.
Thumbnail: the dam on the left is an AI illustration (ChatGPT), not a photograph of the GERD; the right panels are Landsat 8 (USGS), 9 Feb 2020 and 8 Jan 2026, water (MNDWI > 0) tinted blue and grown 2 px for legibility. Headline font: Anton (SIL OFL 1.1).

